  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access — Phishing: Spearphishing Link (delivery)", "technique": "T1566.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 09:02:11 CEST, Wazuh fired rule 91400 'Spearphish email from lookalike/homoglyph domain' three times within ~4 minutes. Three lookalike sender domains — corp-helpdesk.online (homoglyph: Cyrillic 'o' U+043E substituted for Latin 'o'), corp-it-update.click and corp-security-alert.online — sent emails to three corp.local recipients (john.smith, laura.taylor, m.lefebvre). The mail subjects ('Action Required: Verify Your Corp Account', 'URGENT: Password Expiry Notice') use authority + urgency social-engineering levers. The SMTP relay used EHLO mail.corp-helpdesk.online and connected from 10.108.57.10.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule.id 91400 fired with rule.groups containing 'phishing' and 'mail'; rule.mitre.id T1566.002 attached automatically. Three distinct rule.description matches in a 4-minute window against three internal recipients = a coordinated spearphishing wave, not a single stray spam message.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Initial Access — Phishing: Spearphishing Link (credential-harvest URL)", "technique": "T1566.002", "what_happened": "At 09:03:40 CEST, Wazuh fired rule 91401 'Credential harvest link embedded in email body' on the same three emails. Each email body contained a per-recipient credential-harvest URL of the form http://10.108.57.10/portal/<user>/auth?token=<hex>&src=helpdesk — a per-user token in the URL is the hallmark of TARGETED (spear) phishing rather than bulk phishing. A second wave at 09:11 used a /reset?tkn=<hex>&ref=security-alert variant with X-Priority:1 urgency headers.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule.id 91401 fired with rule.mitre.id T1566.002, correlated to the same three recipient mailboxes as step 1 within the same incident window. The per-user token in the URL path links the harvest link directly to the targeted-delivery event.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:03:40+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access — Phishing: Spearphishing Link (suspected user click)", "technique": "T1566.002", "what_happened": "Beginning 09:07:55 CEST, Suricata logged outbound HTTP from recipient endpoint 10.20.14.61 (john.smith) to data.dstip 10.108.57.10 with data.http.url containing /portal/john.smith/auth — i.e. the exact per-user harvest URL from the email john.smith received. This is the click: the user followed the link from the phishing email to the attacker-controlled credential-harvest page. Suricata also logged a DNS lookup for corp-helpdesk.online from the same endpoint immediately prior.", "chain_reason": "Suricata HTTP event with data.dstip = the step-1 SMTP relay IP and data.http.url containing the step-2 per-user harvest path, sourced from the recipient endpoint. The URL-path username matches the recipient identity = the delivery alert and the network click are the same victim.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:07:55+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Credential Access — suspected credential submission", "technique": "T1566.002", "what_happened": "At 09:08:20 CEST, ~25 seconds after the GET to the harvest page, Suricata logged an HTTP POST from 10.20.14.61 to data.dstip 10.108.57.10 with data.http.url /portal/john.smith/auth and a non-zero request body length — consistent with john.smith submitting credentials into the fake helpdesk login form. No follow-on endpoint payload execution was observed on 10.20.14.61, so this remains a credential-harvest (not malware-delivery) phishing chain.", "chain_reason": "Suricata HTTP event with data.http.http_method POST to the same harvest URL and host that received the GET in step 3, ~25s later — the GET-then-POST timing on a credential page is the canonical 'page loaded, form submitted' sequence.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:08:20+0200"}
  ],
